{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8b7b2fef-0218-4f50-abf2-de265dd6b35e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nWednesday, September 16, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n282 compiled articles (210 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n## Sessions\\n\\n## Memory Maintenance\\n\\n### Session (08:42)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` and `/dev-sync` on the Mac; resolved four backup errors and confirmed all repos clean.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Initial backup run: 4 errors, 78 jobs, CRASHBOX SSD mounted. Two were merge-needed repos (`dotclaude`, `global-memory` had new Windows commits) \\u2014 merged and pushed. One was an unexplained `.codex` \\u2192 SSD copy failure. One was 6 repos created on Windows yesterday missing from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`: `PP_Recruiting`, `PythonScripts_2024`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, `Supervisor_Reviews`, `VALD_Performance`, `VALD_Release`.\\n- Agent recommended classifying the 6 as work repos based on Baylor-sounding names; user corrected: **they are personal**.\\n- Sync pulled Windows commits into 5 repos: `_INBOX`, `agent-skills`, `CLI_Dev`, `LibraryProject`, `ZoteroInjestion`. No conflicts, no missing clones. `LibraryProject`'s vendored `ZoteroInjestion` copy was one commit behind and was fast-forwarded.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- 6 new repos added to `backup-manifest.json` as **personal**, destination SSD + GitHub (user overrode the work-repo suggestion). Committed and pushed.\\n- Moved the 4 stale SSD skill folders to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` rather than deleting \\u2014 copies also exist in the skills repo on GitHub.\\n- Archived and cleared `.errors.log` only after a clean run (0 errors, 86 jobs); pushed.\\n- User approved documenting the symlink-vs-directory fix in the `/backup` skill notes.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **Root cause of the `.codex` SSD copy failure:** on the Mac, Codex skills `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout` are now **symlinks**, but the SSD backup still held them as real directories. rsync cannot replace an existing directory with a symlink, so the copy failed with no useful error. This recurs any time a skill converts to a symlink \\u2014 stale real directories must be moved aside on the SSD.\\n- Job-count sanity check confirmed again: SSD mounted \\u2192 high job count (78, then 86); single-digit `okCount` with `status: OK` means the SSD was absent and working-tree capture silently skipped.\\n- Backup manifest classification is not inferable from repo names \\u2014 Baylor-sounding names can still be personal repos. Ask.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- **Add the symlink/stale-directory gotcha to the `/backup` skill** (user said yes) \\u2014 document that `.codex` copy failures trace to real directories on the SSD shadowing new symlinks, and the fix is to move them to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-<date>/`.\\n- `ZoteroInjestion` `docs/STATUS.md` has **no Next section** and still carries an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom \\u2014 needs reconciling.\\n- Optional cleanup: delete `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` later.\\n- Carried over, still open: Windows backup fix (`FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md` scheduled-task triggers); confirm the 913-file skill audit was intentional.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":912},"type":"attachment","uuid":"51bf7eef-bff2-44c9-8416-10c569141e3c","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:28.689Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"51bf7eef-bff2-44c9-8416-10c569141e3c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8b7b2fef-0218-4f50-abf2-de265dd6b35e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\\\"sharpened, not soothed\\\").\\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads\\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active job projects w/ charters, cap 5 (Fall only), timer via ritual-check. Clear nags: `portfolio.py mark-done`. →Tier A prioritization-slate.\\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **Parked 9/14**: Baseball profiles, Acro pass builder (waiting on coach), 6 more job projects. Kinexon VB DONE (Wenger, McGuyre use it).\\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. Micah silent ~4wk; ITS pending. Inventory: only VB out; refresh 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Status report**: #2 send by 9/18, #3 by 9/25, then biweekly to 12/18 (timer checkpoints). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **Open PRs**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1.\\n- **GPS pull (9/13)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; Windows step OFF. NEXT: rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **Win refresh task (9/13)**: ForcePlate step broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which machine owns forceplate/perch DBs first. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: Wk3 keys + profile v2 on Drive. EPA = PFF current; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\\n\\n[2026-09-16T07:24:03-0500] gps-pull: error exit=4 roster fetch failed (Catapult API error 0: network error: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='connect-us.catapultsports.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /api/v6/athletes (Caused by NameResolution — see /Users/kristinarash/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/gps_report/logs/last_run.json\\n[2026-09-16T12:46:41.423Z] memsearch-sync: index exit 1: Traceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"/Users/kristinarash/.local/share/uv/tools/memsearch/lib/python3.12/site-packages/memsearch/embeddings/onnx.py\\\", line 31, in __init__\\n    import onnxruntime as ort\\n  File \\\"/Users/kristinarash/.local/share/uv/tools/memsearch/lib/python3.12/site-packages/onnxr\\n[2026-09-16 09:04] daily-backup: 2 error(s): repo-diverged:global-memory, repo-diverged:dotclaude - see ~/.claude/scripts/logs/daily-backup.log\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4330 chars, updated 2026-09-16 11:53)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":915},"type":"attachment","uuid":"adc67424-3df7-4298-9b9e-f6c1b4386672","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:28.690Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"adc67424-3df7-4298-9b9e-f6c1b4386672","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8b7b2fef-0218-4f50-abf2-de265dd6b35e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":1299},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9180c67d-4477-4ba5-8310-073a607a312f","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:29.074Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"9180c67d-4477-4ba5-8310-073a607a312f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8b7b2fef-0218-4f50-abf2-de265dd6b35e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).","stdout":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/scripts/check-config.sh\"","durationMs":1300},"type":"attachment","uuid":"710b942c-635b-4293-a5d3-f55ba147ea03","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:29.075Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart:startup hook success: /last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"710b942c-635b-4293-a5d3-f55ba147ea03","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8b7b2fef-0218-4f50-abf2-de265dd6b35e","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":1483},"type":"attachment","uuid":"62316546-04f8-44e5-85d6-7b7f77a2110e","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:29.258Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"62316546-04f8-44e5-85d6-7b7f77a2110e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## Today\nWednesday, September 16, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n282 compiled articles (210 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (08:42)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` and `/dev-sync` on the Mac; resolved four backup errors and confirmed all repos clean.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Initial backup run: 4 errors, 78 jobs, CRASHBOX SSD mounted. Two were merge-needed repos (`dotclaude`, `global-memory` had new Windows commits) — merged and pushed. One was an unexplained `.codex` → SSD copy failure. One was 6 repos created on Windows yesterday missing from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`: `PP_Recruiting`, `PythonScripts_2024`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, `Supervisor_Reviews`, `VALD_Performance`, `VALD_Release`.\n- Agent recommended classifying the 6 as work repos based on Baylor-sounding names; user corrected: **they are personal**.\n- Sync pulled Windows commits into 5 repos: `_INBOX`, `agent-skills`, `CLI_Dev`, `LibraryProject`, `ZoteroInjestion`. No conflicts, no missing clones. `LibraryProject`'s vendored `ZoteroInjestion` copy was one commit behind and was fast-forwarded.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- 6 new repos added to `backup-manifest.json` as **personal**, destination SSD + GitHub (user overrode the work-repo suggestion). Committed and pushed.\n- Moved the 4 stale SSD skill folders to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` rather than deleting — copies also exist in the skills repo on GitHub.\n- Archived and cleared `.errors.log` only after a clean run (0 errors, 86 jobs); pushed.\n- User approved documenting the symlink-vs-directory fix in the `/backup` skill notes.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Root cause of the `.codex` SSD copy failure:** on the Mac, Codex skills `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout` are now **symlinks**, but the SSD backup still held them as real directories. rsync cannot replace an existing directory with a symlink, so the copy failed with no useful error. This recurs any time a skill converts to a symlink — stale real directories must be moved aside on the SSD.\n- Job-count sanity check confirmed again: SSD mounted → high job count (78, then 86); single-digit `okCount` with `status: OK` means the SSD was absent and working-tree capture silently skipped.\n- Backup manifest classification is not inferable from repo names — Baylor-sounding names can still be personal repos. Ask.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Add the symlink/stale-directory gotcha to the `/backup` skill** (user said yes) — document that `.codex` copy failures trace to real directories on the SSD shadowing new symlinks, and the fix is to move them to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-<date>/`.\n- `ZoteroInjestion` `docs/STATUS.md` has **no Next section** and still carries an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom — needs reconciling.\n- Optional cleanup: delete `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` later.\n- Carried over, still open: Windows backup fix (`FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md` scheduled-task triggers); confirm the 913-file skill audit was intentional.\n","## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active job projects w/ charters, cap 5 (Fall only), timer via ritual-check. Clear nags: `portfolio.py mark-done`. →Tier A prioritization-slate.\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Parked 9/14**: Baseball profiles, Acro pass builder (waiting on coach), 6 more job projects. Kinexon VB DONE (Wenger, McGuyre use it).\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. Micah silent ~4wk; ITS pending. Inventory: only VB out; refresh 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Status report**: #2 send by 9/18, #3 by 9/25, then biweekly to 12/18 (timer checkpoints). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Open PRs**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1.\n- **GPS pull (9/13)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; Windows step OFF. NEXT: rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Win refresh task (9/13)**: ForcePlate step broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which machine owns forceplate/perch DBs first. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: Wk3 keys + profile v2 on Drive. EPA = PFF current; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n[2026-09-16T07:24:03-0500] gps-pull: error exit=4 roster fetch failed (Catapult API error 0: network error: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='connect-us.catapultsports.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /api/v6/athletes (Caused by NameResolution — see /Users/kristinarash/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/gps_report/logs/last_run.json\n[2026-09-16T12:46:41.423Z] memsearch-sync: index exit 1: Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/Users/kristinarash/.local/share/uv/tools/memsearch/lib/python3.12/site-packages/memsearch/embeddings/onnx.py\", line 31, in __init__\n    import onnxruntime as ort\n  File \"/Users/kristinarash/.local/share/uv/tools/memsearch/lib/python3.12/site-packages/onnxr\n[2026-09-16 09:04] daily-backup: 2 error(s): repo-diverged:global-memory, repo-diverged:dotclaude - see ~/.claude/scripts/logs/daily-backup.log\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4330 chars, updated 2026-09-16 11:53)","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"dec00ed4-3b32-409b-b12c-2a3c62d01a2c","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:43:29.260Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## Today\nWednesday, September 16, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n282 compiled articles (210 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (08:42)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` and `/dev-sync` on the Mac; resolved four backup errors and confirmed all repos clean.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Initial backup run: 4 errors, 78 jobs, CRASHBOX SSD mounted. Two were merge-needed repos (`dotclaude`, `global-memory` had new Windows commits) — merged and pushed. One was an unexplained `.codex` → SSD copy failure. One was 6 repos created on Windows yesterday missing from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`: `PP_Recruiting`, `PythonScripts_2024`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, `Supervisor_Reviews`, `VALD_Performance`, `VALD_Release`.\n- Agent recommended classifying the 6 as work repos based on Baylor-sounding names; user corrected: **they are personal**.\n- Sync pulled Windows commits into 5 repos: `_INBOX`, `agent-skills`, `CLI_Dev`, `LibraryProject`, `ZoteroInjestion`. No conflicts, no missing clones. `LibraryProject`'s vendored `ZoteroInjestion` copy was one commit behind and was fast-forwarded.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- 6 new repos added to `backup-manifest.json` as **personal**, destination SSD + GitHub (user overrode the work-repo suggestion). Committed and pushed.\n- Moved the 4 stale SSD skill folders to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` rather than deleting — copies also exist in the skills repo on GitHub.\n- Archived and cleared `.errors.log` only after a clean run (0 errors, 86 jobs); pushed.\n- User approved documenting the symlink-vs-directory fix in the `/backup` skill notes.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Root cause of the `.codex` SSD copy failure:** on the Mac, Codex skills `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout` are now **symlinks**, but the SSD backup still held them as real directories. rsync cannot replace an existing directory with a symlink, so the copy failed with no useful error. This recurs any time a skill converts to a symlink — stale real directories must be moved aside on the SSD.\n- Job-count sanity check confirmed again: SSD mounted → high job count (78, then 86); single-digit `okCount` with `status: OK` means the SSD was absent and working-tree capture silently skipped.\n- Backup manifest classification is not inferable from repo names — Baylor-sounding names can still be personal repos. Ask.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Add the symlink/stale-directory gotcha to the `/backup` skill** (user said yes) — document that `.codex` copy failures trace to real directories on the SSD shadowing new symlinks, and the fix is to move them to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-<date>/`.\n- `ZoteroInjestion` `docs/STATUS.md` has **no Next section** and still carries an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom — needs reconciling.\n- Optional cleanup: delete `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` later.\n- Carried over, still open: Windows backup fix (`FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md` scheduled-task triggers); confirm the 913-file skill audit was intentional.\n\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Storage = Google Drive only (OneDrive dropped 9/13); DuckDB stays local — cloud sync locks it.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active job projects w/ charters, cap 5 (Fall only), timer via ritual-check. Clear nags: `portfolio.py mark-done`. →Tier A prioritization-slate.\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Parked 9/14**: Baseball profiles, Acro pass builder (waiting on coach), 6 more job projects. Kinexon VB DONE (Wenger, McGuyre use it).\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. Micah silent ~4wk; ITS pending. Inventory: only VB out; refresh 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Status report**: #2 send by 9/18, #3 by 9/25, then biweekly to 12/18 (timer checkpoints). →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Open PRs**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1.\n- **GPS pull (9/13)**: LIVE Mac 06:30; Windows step OFF. NEXT: rotate token. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **Win refresh task (9/13)**: ForcePlate step broken, Perch untested. DECIDE which machine owns forceplate/perch DBs first. →`_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: Wk3 keys + profile v2 on Drive. EPA = PFF current; CFBD PPA cross-check only. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both `.claude/hooks/` and `.codex/hooks/`. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n[2026-09-16T07:24:03-0500] gps-pull: error exit=4 roster fetch failed (Catapult API error 0: network error: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='connect-us.catapultsports.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /api/v6/athletes (Caused by NameResolution — see /Users/kristinarash/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/gps_report/logs/last_run.json\n[2026-09-16T12:46:41.423Z] memsearch-sync: index exit 1: Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/Users/kristinarash/.local/share/uv/tools/memsearch/lib/python3.12/site-packages/memsearch/embeddings/onnx.py\", line 31, in __init__\n    import onnxruntime as ort\n  File \"/Users/kristinarash/.local/share/uv/tools/memsearch/lib/python3.12/site-packages/onnxr\n[2026-09-16 09:04] daily-backup: 2 error(s): repo-diverged:global-memory, repo-diverged:dotclaude - see ~/.claude/scripts/logs/daily-backup.log\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4330 chars, updated 2026-09-16 11:53)\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\n</system-reminder>"}],"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
