{"parentUuid":"63b7038e-a1fd-402c-a4b1-9a2db7e8b041","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf82rDxFjUtcew2ZURrHS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YESGXyUaBh2KxcHwaNZwXY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/sleep-scheduler; cat docs/STATUS.md; echo; echo \"--- HANDOFF\"; cat docs/handoffs/2026-09-16-itinerary-model-and-csv-input.md; git log -6 --format='%ci %an %s'","description":"Read sleep-scheduler STATUS and newest handoff"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3742,"cache_read_input_tokens":81811,"output_tokens":264,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":75},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3742,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":264,"cache_read_input_tokens":81811,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3742},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01YESGXyUaBh2KxcHwaNZwXY":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/sleep-scheduler; cat docs/STATUS.md; echo; echo \"--- HANDOFF\"; cat docs/handoffs/2026-09-16-itinerary-model-and-csv-input.md; git log -6 --format='%ci %an %s'","description":"Read sleep-scheduler STATUS and newest handoff"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cf82rDNXCCHvpyB8PkQ7X","attributionSkill":"dev-sync","type":"assistant","uuid":"65069d8a-bfac-45d8-b285-1013b13df43e","timestamp":"2026-09-17T00:03:52.239Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"65069d8a-bfac-45d8-b285-1013b13df43e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"1a9e6a61-377c-4fad-af18-9476f244b7f0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YESGXyUaBh2KxcHwaNZwXY","type":"tool_result","content":"# STATUS\n\nRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n\n## Current state\n\nRelease 1 design confirmed 2026-09-15 (decisions table in\n`SLEEP-SCHEDULER-SPEC.md`). Package 1 (calendar core) and Package 1b\n(game-weekend model) are implemented test-first on branch\n`worktree-calendar-core`, PR #1 merged to `main` 2026-09-16. Package 2 (staff CSV input) and\nPackage 3 (one-page HTML comparison, plus `export`) were built 2026-09-16. 210 tests pass; run `python -m pytest`\nfrom the repo root. Records: `docs/handoffs/2026-09-16-itinerary-model-and-csv-input.md` (this session),\n`docs/handoffs/2026-09-15-game-weekend-model.md`\n(model build, itinerary review).\n\n**2026-09-16: the model was reshaped for the real itineraries.** The six\nitinerary questions were adopted at their recommended answers and **confirmed\nby the user the same day**; see the table under \"Decisions from the first real\nitineraries\" in the spec's \"Protected sleep window rule\" section. Built\ntest-first:\n\n- **Clock-time anchoring.** `weekend.ClockTime(day, \"HH:MM\")` (day counted from\n  game day, `-1` = Friday) can place a template entry's edges or a night's\n  curfew/wake instead of a kickoff offset. An entry uses one anchoring for both\n  edges. Clock entries carry no `KickoffAnchor`, so a kickoff change moves\n  Saturday and reports nothing for Friday. Offset overrides must match the\n  entry's anchoring.\n- **Window start rule (closes old open question 7).** Only an obligation that\n  begins before the current start pushes it. Later ones stay inside the window\n  as reported conflicts. A breakfast window opening before the wake-up call now\n  clips the night instead of erasing it.\n- **Nights without a curfew.** `NightMarker(curfew=None, released_by=<event id>)`\n  starts at that event's end + `AssumptionSet.release_to_bed_minutes`\n  (source `release:<id>`). The CLI prints and labels that assumption.\n- **File format.** Optional keys `<edge>_clock`, `released_by`,\n  `release_to_bed_minutes`; `FORMAT_VERSION` still 1, old fixtures load\n  unchanged, and the CLI example output is unchanged.\n\nChecked against the first real road itinerary: with breakfast as its check-in,\nFriday's window matches the Drive review and is set by curfew; as a whole\nbreakfast block the night is clipped with a visible conflict instead of\nerased. Real times and results stay on Drive, not here.\n\n**Package 2, staff CSV input (2026-09-16).** `itinerary_csv.py` reads one\nweek's itinerary CSV (settings block + `kind,day,start,end,title,at,arrive_at,\nmovable,releases,id` rows) into a game weekend and writes one back. Format and\nrules: spec section \"Staff CSV format\". The CLI takes `.csv` or `.json` on\neither side. Import refuses unknown kinds, zero-length items, duplicate ids,\nand badly bounded nights, listing every bad row. `itineraries/` is gitignored for\nreal filled-in copies.\n\n**Package 3, one-page HTML comparison (2026-09-16).** `report_html.py`;\n`python -m sleep_scheduler <baseline> <candidate> --html out.html` prints the\nterminal report and writes a self-contained page (inline CSS, no scripts or\nexternal resources) that prints to one Letter page. It covers: per-night\nresult with baseline and candidate status against target (words, not colour\nonly), what changed by title, a \"Needs sign-off\" box for non-levers, what set\neach window edge in plain language, conflicts, and labelled assumptions.\nChecked by printing a real comparison to PDF with headless Chrome: one page.\n\nThe first two real staff itineraries (Auburn, PVAMU) live on Drive, not in Git:\n`G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\99_UNSORTED\\From_Desktop\\TECHNOLOGY\\FAST\\GAME_WEEK_ITINERARIES\\2026\\`,\nwith extracted times and offsets in `extracted-timeline-review.md` there.\n\nWorking example from CSV (synthetic home game with a Friday hotel):\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=src python -m sleep_scheduler \\\n  fixtures/home-night-hotel-baseline.csv fixtures/home-night-hotel-candidate.csv\n```\n\nMeetings 20:45–21:45 instead of 20:00–21:00 push Friday's start from the 22:00\ncurfew to 22:15: 12h00m → 11h45m, −15m, no flags. Saturday is 9h from release\n(game ends 23:00 + 60 min) to the assumed 09:00 wake.\n\nWorking example from JSON — the Friday early-kickoff comparison:\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=src python -m sleep_scheduler \\\n  fixtures/friday-early-kickoff-baseline.json fixtures/friday-early-kickoff-candidate.json\n```\n\nAn early road kickoff: 11:00 MT Saturday, home in Central time. In the baseline\nFriday meetings run 22:00–23:00 MT, so with a 30-minute wind-down the Friday\nwindow starts at 23:30 instead of the 23:00 curfew: 6h30m. Moving meetings an\nhour earlier lets curfew set the start: 7h00m, +30m. Thursday (8h) and Saturday\n(10h, to an assumed 09:00 wake) are unchanged, and only a movable event changed,\nso there are no flags. Every offset, curfew, and wake time is synthetic. This\nfixture still uses offset anchoring and a Saturday curfew; the synthetic\n`home_night_template()` in `tests/synthetic.py` shows the itinerary-shaped form\n(clock Friday, breakfast check-in, released Saturday).\n\nModules, all under `src/sleep_scheduler/`:\n\n| Module | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `temporal.py` | Local wall time + IANA zone → UTC instant; half-open intervals; merge/subtract |\n| `schedule.py` | Event (optional `KickoffAnchor`), ProtectedSleepWindow (edge sources), AssumptionSet (`saturday_night_wake`, `release_to_bed_minutes`), Scenario |\n| `opportunity.py` | Spec steps 1–8; conflicts; comparison with changed, removed, added, and fixed-event changes |\n| `weekend.py` | Kickoff bands, `ClockTime`, templates, `build_weekend`, the sleep window rule, `evaluate_weekend` / `compare_weekends`, flags |\n| `adapter.py` | Forecast seam; the only adapter returns a status, never a number |\n| `persistence.py` | Scenario JSON round-trip; game-weekend files (inputs only, rebuilt on load) |\n| `itinerary_csv.py` | Staff CSV in and out; import validation; anchoring from day + time form |\n| `cli.py` | `python -m sleep_scheduler <baseline> <candidate>` (each `.csv` or `.json`) |\n\n## Design confirmed (2026-09-15)\n\nThe full answer table is in `SLEEP-SCHEDULER-SPEC.md` under \"Release 1 design\ndecisions\". In short: Release 1 compares Baylor football game weekends\n(Thursday evening to Sunday midday) for **Friday practice/meeting times against\nSaturday kickoff** and **road-game departures, including the late return**. It\nreports sleep opportunity per night for one group (the travel party, or the\nwhole roster at home). Applied Performance runs it locally: CSV in, one-page\nHTML comparison out. There is no Sunday lift (player off day), and subgroups\nare out of Release 1.\n\n## Next\n\n1. **Real baselines are on Drive (2026-09-16).** Eric verified the extracted\n   times; `game1-auburn-baseline.csv` and `game2-pvamu-baseline.csv` sit next to\n   the staff PDFs in the GAME_WEEK_ITINERARIES\\2026 folder and run cleanly.\n   Entry conventions used there: check-ins as 10-minute items ending at the\n   \"by\" time; \"available\", some-players, and who-unclear items `optional`;\n   missing ends run to the next item (daytime only); Saturday released by the\n   facility bus (road) or post-game (home).\n2. **First real candidate rejected (2026-09-16).** Against 9h the road Saturday\n   night was the only night short, by less than half an hour. The candidate cut\n   post-game to leave earlier; Eric ruled post-game fixed (media obligations,\n   players changing, equipment collection). The CSV and page on Drive are\n   titled REJECTED. With post-game fixed, the road return after an afternoon\n   kickoff has no lever left inside the model except the travel legs\n   themselves (airport transfer, landing-to-facility bus). The shortfall is\n   smaller than the uncertainty in the 60-min release assumption, so treat\n   that night as at target, not a problem to fix.\n3. **Watch for the cases the model is for:** a later road kickoff (return\n   after midnight), an early kickoff (released-night floor, open question 4),\n   or Friday evening items running past curfew minus wind-down (both real\n   Fridays have zero slack).\n4. **Each new game week:** enter the itinerary CSV on Drive, run it against\n   the 9h target, and build a candidate only when a night is short.\n5. **Kickoff what-ifs (2026-09-16).** `python -m sleep_scheduler export <weekend>\n   <out.csv>` writes game-day rows as `K` offsets (refuses to overwrite). Copy\n   the export, change only `kickoff`, compare. First use, on Drive: the real\n   road trip with a hypothetical night kickoff. Saturday night falls several\n   hours short, while Friday gains only because the wake-up call follows\n   kickoff, which a real night itinerary would not do. Caveat: the Saturday\n   timeline is scaled from an afternoon itinerary. For late returns the\n   binding edge is the assumed Sunday wake. Sunday is not planned beyond the\n   medical check-in (Eric, 2026-09-16), so there is no Sunday lever either:\n   a late road kickoff is a cost to report, not a schedule to fix.\n\n- Deferred out of Release 1: forecast engine, subgroups/non-travel,\n  `SleepObservation`, candidate generation (CP-SAT), archive migration, the\n  athlete mobile itinerary, hosting and roles.\n- `AGENTS.md` still carries an unfolded `.from-windows` merge block (a stale\n  pre-interview copy); safe to delete once someone confirms nothing in it is\n  wanted.\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. (Resolved 2026-09-16: the six itinerary decisions are confirmed.)\n2. A home game with a Friday hotel uses `at=\"away\"` (game zone = home zone) to\n   mark the Friday window as a hotel window. Fine for now; revisit if a\n   template needs a hotel in the home zone on a road trip.\n3. Should the first obligation after the start ever push it (a travel leg\n   departing after curfew)? Under a curfew it is now a conflict. Real itineraries\n   put no travel after curfew, and Saturday uses release instead. Revisit only\n   if a real case appears.\n4. **Released nights have no floor.** `released_by` assumes a late release. For\n   an early home kickoff (game over ~15:00, +45m) the window line would print a\n   mid-afternoon start while the table clips it to the 18:00 reporting start\n   (~15h). Options: allow `curfew` and `released_by` together (start = the later\n   of the two), or give the early band a clock-time Saturday curfew. Decide when\n   an early-kickoff itinerary arrives; do not draft an early template before.\n5. (Resolved 2026-09-16: target 9h opportunity, 30-min wind-down, 60-min\n   release to bed, 09:00 Sunday wake. Eric deferred the call; evidence in the\n   spec under \"Assumption set for real weekends\".)\n\nStill needed from staff: verification of the extracted itinerary times, an\nearly-kickoff itinerary, and confirmation of the 30-minute wind-down buffer.\n\n## Gotchas\n\n- **Real itineraries never go in Git.** They are team schedules (AGENTS.md\n  archive boundary). Keep them and anything derived from them on Drive,\n  including times and night results quoted in STATUS or handoffs. The\n  synthetic home template copies their *shape*, never their times.\n- **Post-game is not compressible** (Eric, 2026-09-16: media, players change,\n  equipment). Enter it as a non-movable item; never build a candidate that\n  shortens it.\n- Real comparison pages and any PDFs printed from them go on Drive, never in\n  the repo or a published artifact.\n- `zoneinfo` does not refuse impossible local times; `temporal.LocalTime`\n  raises on DST gaps and ambiguous times. Do not bypass it. `ClockTime`\n  resolves through it.\n- `Interval` equality ignores zone provenance on purpose; round-trip tests\n  compare serialized dicts to check zones.\n- Events are mandatory **by default**; only `schedule.OPTIONAL_KINDS` are\n  ignored. Never reintroduce a mandatory whitelist. \"Treatment available\"-style\n  items must be entered with an optional kind.\n- Curfew and wake-up are `NightMarker`s, never Events. A marker has exactly one\n  of `curfew` or `released_by`.\n- The wind-down buffer **is** `pre_bed_minutes`; do not add a second field.\n  `release_to_bed_minutes` is different: it *replaces* the buffer at a\n  released night's start.\n- `morning_minutes` widens the block before every obligation and can clip the\n  window before a wake-up call; fixtures use 0.\n- A staff end override is settled before obligations are checked; a start\n  override is applied after.\n- Night reporting intervals are fixed by date (18:00–12:00 in the night's zone,\n  dated from the start before any push). Never derive them from the proposed\n  window.\n- Kickoff-anchored events compare by offset; a kickoff change is one\n  `kickoff_shift`. Clock events compare by instant. Change detection covers\n  start, end, removed, and added.\n- `ClockTime.day` counts from the kickoff date **on the stadium clock**.\n- Game-weekend files store inputs and are rebuilt on load; never add resolved\n  instants. Newer keys are optional, so `FORMAT_VERSION` is 1.\n- The CLI reads game-weekend files only; `--window` is gone.\n- Itinerary \"EST/CST\" in September means daylight time; use IANA zones.\n- **Editing CSVs in Excel:** prefer Save As -> \"CSV UTF-8\". Import also\n  accepts Excel's plain CSV (cp1252) and its `10:45:00` / `10:45 AM` time\n  rewrites, but check that times survived and that `K-4:15` cells were not\n  turned into dates or formulas.\n- The `game` row must start at the `kickoff` setting; import refuses a\n  mismatch rather than shifting every clock-typed Saturday row.\n- The synthetic fixtures were generated once from `tests/synthetic.py`; the\n  generator was not kept. Edit the JSON directly.\n\n--- HANDOFF\n# Handoff: itinerary-shaped model and staff CSV input, 2026-09-16\n\n## What was worked on and why\n\nPicked up from `2026-09-15-game-weekend-model.md`. The first real staff itineraries (Auburn, PVAMU) showed the template model did not fit them. Curfew and Friday practice/meetings are clock times regardless of kickoff. A breakfast window erased Friday night. Saturday has no curfew. The session reshaped the model, got the user's confirmation of six itinerary decisions, and built Package 2 (staff CSV input), all test-first. 133 → 194 tests.\n\n## Decisions made (with reasoning)\n\n- **Six itinerary decisions, confirmed by the user 2026-09-16** (spec table \"Decisions from the first real itineraries\"):\n  1. Friday curfew is a clock time.\n  2. Friday practice and meetings are clock times; kickoff drives Saturday.\n  3. Breakfast counts as its check-in; \"available\" items are optional.\n  4. Saturday has no curfew and starts 45 minutes after release.\n  5. There is no Thursday night until an itinerary gives a curfew.\n  6. Neutral-site games are road games.\n\n  The spec's Q5 and Q6 rows were amended to match.\n- **Window start rule:** only an obligation that begins before the current start pushes it. Later ones stay inside the window as conflicts. This fixed the breakfast erasure (on the real road itinerary, Friday went from no window to a clipped window with a visible conflict, or a full window set by curfew with breakfast entered as its check-in). It also closed the old open question 7.\n- **Released nights:** `NightMarker(curfew=None, released_by=id)` plus `AssumptionSet.release_to_bed_minutes`. It replaces the wind-down buffer at that edge rather than adding to it, and it is labelled as an assumption in the report.\n- **Clock anchoring:** `ClockTime(day, HH:MM)`. Clock entries carry no kickoff anchor, so a kickoff change doesn't report Friday events as moved. An entry must place both edges the same way, and overrides must match the entry's anchoring, so an entry can't be quietly re-anchored.\n- **CSV anchoring has no column.** A `K` offset follows kickoff. A clock time before game day stays on the clock. A clock time on game day or later becomes an offset from the file's kickoff. This encodes decision 2 directly.\n- **Import refuses unknown kinds.** The model deliberately treats unknown kinds as mandatory, so a typo would silently block sleep.\n- **The game row must start at `kickoff`.** Otherwise a typo shifts every clock-typed Saturday row without warning.\n- **Export writes game-day rows as `K` offsets** so a copy re-flows when kickoff changes. It refuses window overrides and extra events rather than writing them lossily.\n- **Real itinerary data stays on Drive.** `itineraries/` is gitignored, and all fixtures use invented times.\n\n## Files created or modified\n\nRepo `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\sleep-scheduler\\.claude\\worktrees\\calendar-core`, branch `worktree-calendar-core`, draft PR #1:\n\n- `src/sleep_scheduler/weekend.py`, `schedule.py`, `persistence.py`, `cli.py`\n- `src/sleep_scheduler/itinerary_csv.py` (new)\n- `tests/test_clock_anchoring.py`, `tests/test_itinerary_csv.py` (new); `tests/synthetic.py`, `test_windows.py`, `test_weekend_compare.py`, `test_weekend_file.py`, `test_cli.py`\n- `fixtures/home-night-hotel-baseline.csv`, `home-night-hotel-candidate.csv`, `road-afternoon-baseline.csv` (new, synthetic)\n- `SLEEP-SCHEDULER-SPEC.md` (window rule, confirmed decisions, \"Staff CSV format\"), `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `docs/STATUS.md`, `.gitignore`\n- `docs/handoffs/2026-09-16-itinerary-model-and-csv-input.md` (this file)\n\nOutside the repo: `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` (Sleep scheduler thread). Nothing was written to Drive this session.\n\n## Later the same day: real weekends, target, HTML page, export\n\n- **Eric verified the extracted times.** Real baselines were entered as CSVs **on Drive only** (`G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\99_UNSORTED\\From_Desktop\\TECHNOLOGY\\FAST\\GAME_WEEK_ITINERARIES\\2026\\`): `game1-auburn-baseline.csv` and `game2-pvamu-baseline.csv`. Entry conventions are in STATUS Next 1.\n- **Assumption set decided (Eric deferred the call):** 9h opportunity target, 30-min wind-down, 60-min release to bed, 09:00 Sunday wake. Reasoning: athlete sleep efficiency is about 81%, so 8h in bed is about 6.5h asleep. Evidence is in the spec under \"Assumption set for real weekends\".\n- **Package 3 built:** `src/sleep_scheduler/report_html.py`, `tests/test_report_html.py`, and a `--html PATH` CLI option. The page is self-contained and prints to one Letter page (checked with headless Chrome).\n- **First real candidate rejected.** It moved the road post-game departure earlier; Eric ruled post-game fixed (media obligations, players changing, equipment collection). Q7 in the spec is amended, and the Drive files are titled REJECTED. The remaining road Saturday shortfall is within the uncertainty of the release assumption.\n- **Export command:** `python -m sleep_scheduler export <weekend> <out.csv>` (in `cli.py`, tests in `tests/test_cli.py`). First use, on Drive and marked HYPOTHETICAL: the road trip with a night kickoff. Saturday night falls several hours short. After a late return, the binding edge is the assumed Sunday wake, so the levers are on Sunday (no morning obligations, or flying home Sunday).\n- **Correction:** real times had been quoted in STATUS, the spec, and this handoff. They were replaced with generic wording (`a04882a`). Earlier commits still contain them in the private repo's history; removing them would need a force-push, which was not done.\n\nAdditional repo files this session: `src/sleep_scheduler/report_html.py`, `tests/test_report_html.py`, `README.md`. Drive files: the two baselines, `game1-auburn-baseline-kickoff-relative.csv`, `game1-auburn-candidate-depart-30-earlier.csv` (REJECTED), `game1-auburn-comparison-depart-30-earlier.html` (REJECTED), `game1-auburn-whatif-1930-kickoff.csv`, and `game1-auburn-whatif-1930-kickoff-comparison.html`.\n\n## Next actions\n\n1. When a night-kickoff road game or an early-kickoff itinerary arrives, enter it on Drive and run it. Those are the cases the tool is for.\n2. (Closed: Eric does not plan Sunday beyond the medical check-in, so Sunday options are not pursued.)\n3. Each game week: enter the itinerary CSV, run it against 9h, and build a candidate only when a night is short and a real lever exists.\n\n## Mid-flight\n\n- Released nights have no earliest start; an early home kickoff would start Saturday mid-afternoon. Decide when an early-kickoff itinerary arrives (STATUS open question 4).\n- PR #1 merged to `main` at Eric's request (2026-09-16).\n- Old commits contain real itinerary times (private repo). Eric decided to keep them; no history rewrite.\n2026-09-16 18:31:41 -0500 Eric Rash Merge pull request #1 from erash11/worktree-calendar-core\n2026-09-16 18:31:24 -0500 Eric Rash Merge remote-tracking branch 'origin/main' into worktree-calendar-core\n2026-09-16 18:30:58 -0500 Eric Rash Record Sunday (medical check-in only) and history decisions; mark PR #1 merged\n2026-09-16 18:25:06 -0500 Eric Rash Closeout 2026-09-16 (later): handoff and STATUS for real weekends, HTML page, export\n2026-09-16 18:01:54 -0500 Eric Rash CLI export command for kickoff-slot what-ifs\n2026-09-16 17:57:46 -0500 Eric Rash Record post-game as fixed; first real candidate rejected","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5fbb165f-af42-40ad-b7a1-685603cb86a7","timestamp":"2026-09-17T00:03:55.216Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# STATUS\n\nRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n\n## Current state\n\nRelease 1 design confirmed 2026-09-15 (decisions table in\n`SLEEP-SCHEDULER-SPEC.md`). Package 1 (calendar core) and Package 1b\n(game-weekend model) are implemented test-first on branch\n`worktree-calendar-core`, PR #1 merged to `main` 2026-09-16. Package 2 (staff CSV input) and\nPackage 3 (one-page HTML comparison, plus `export`) were built 2026-09-16. 210 tests pass; run `python -m pytest`\nfrom the repo root. Records: `docs/handoffs/2026-09-16-itinerary-model-and-csv-input.md` (this session),\n`docs/handoffs/2026-09-15-game-weekend-model.md`\n(model build, itinerary review).\n\n**2026-09-16: the model was reshaped for the real itineraries.** The six\nitinerary questions were adopted at their recommended answers and **confirmed\nby the user the same day**; see the table under \"Decisions from the first real\nitineraries\" in the spec's \"Protected sleep window rule\" section. Built\ntest-first:\n\n- **Clock-time anchoring.** `weekend.ClockTime(day, \"HH:MM\")` (day counted from\n  game day, `-1` = Friday) can place a template entry's edges or a night's\n  curfew/wake instead of a kickoff offset. An entry uses one anchoring for both\n  edges. Clock entries carry no `KickoffAnchor`, so a kickoff change moves\n  Saturday and reports nothing for Friday. Offset overrides must match the\n  entry's anchoring.\n- **Window start rule (closes old open question 7).** Only an obligation that\n  begins before the current start pushes it. Later ones stay inside the window\n  as reported conflicts. A breakfast window opening before the wake-up call now\n  clips the night instead of erasing it.\n- **Nights without a curfew.** `NightMarker(curfew=None, released_by=<event id>)`\n  starts at that event's end + `AssumptionSet.release_to_bed_minutes`\n  (source `release:<id>`). The CLI prints and labels that assumption.\n- **File format.** Optional keys `<edge>_clock`, `released_by`,\n  `release_to_bed_minutes`; `FORMAT_VERSION` still 1, old fixtures load\n  unchanged, and the CLI example output is unchanged.\n\nChecked against the first real road itinerary: with breakfast as its check-in,\nFriday's window matches the Drive review and is set by curfew; as a whole\nbreakfast block the night is clipped with a visible conflict instead of\nerased. Real times and results stay on Drive, not here.\n\n**Package 2, staff CSV input (2026-09-16).** `itinerary_csv.py` reads one\nweek's itinerary CSV (settings block + `kind,day,start,end,title,at,arrive_at,\nmovable,releases,id` rows) into a game weekend and writes one back. Format and\nrules: spec section \"Staff CSV format\". The CLI takes `.csv` or `.json` on\neither side. Import refuses unknown kinds, zero-length items, duplicate ids,\nand badly bounded nights, listing every bad row. `itineraries/` is gitignored for\nreal filled-in copies.\n\n**Package 3, one-page HTML comparison (2026-09-16).** `report_html.py`;\n`python -m sleep_scheduler <baseline> <candidate> --html out.html` prints the\nterminal report and writes a self-contained page (inline CSS, no scripts or\nexternal resources) that prints to one Letter page. It covers: per-night\nresult with baseline and candidate status against target (words, not colour\nonly), what changed by title, a \"Needs sign-off\" box for non-levers, what set\neach window edge in plain language, conflicts, and labelled assumptions.\nChecked by printing a real comparison to PDF with headless Chrome: one page.\n\nThe first two real staff itineraries (Auburn, PVAMU) live on Drive, not in Git:\n`G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\99_UNSORTED\\From_Desktop\\TECHNOLOGY\\FAST\\GAME_WEEK_ITINERARIES\\2026\\`,\nwith extracted times and offsets in `extracted-timeline-review.md` there.\n\nWorking example from CSV (synthetic home game with a Friday hotel):\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=src python -m sleep_scheduler \\\n  fixtures/home-night-hotel-baseline.csv fixtures/home-night-hotel-candidate.csv\n```\n\nMeetings 20:45–21:45 instead of 20:00–21:00 push Friday's start from the 22:00\ncurfew to 22:15: 12h00m → 11h45m, −15m, no flags. Saturday is 9h from release\n(game ends 23:00 + 60 min) to the assumed 09:00 wake.\n\nWorking example from JSON — the Friday early-kickoff comparison:\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=src python -m sleep_scheduler \\\n  fixtures/friday-early-kickoff-baseline.json fixtures/friday-early-kickoff-candidate.json\n```\n\nAn early road kickoff: 11:00 MT Saturday, home in Central time. In the baseline\nFriday meetings run 22:00–23:00 MT, so with a 30-minute wind-down the Friday\nwindow starts at 23:30 instead of the 23:00 curfew: 6h30m. Moving meetings an\nhour earlier lets curfew set the start: 7h00m, +30m. Thursday (8h) and Saturday\n(10h, to an assumed 09:00 wake) are unchanged, and only a movable event changed,\nso there are no flags. Every offset, curfew, and wake time is synthetic. This\nfixture still uses offset anchoring and a Saturday curfew; the synthetic\n`home_night_template()` in `tests/synthetic.py` shows the itinerary-shaped form\n(clock Friday, breakfast check-in, released Saturday).\n\nModules, all under `src/sleep_scheduler/`:\n\n| Module | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `temporal.py` | Local wall time + IANA zone → UTC instant; half-open intervals; merge/subtract |\n| `schedule.py` | Event (optional `KickoffAnchor`), ProtectedSleepWindow (edge sources), AssumptionSet (`saturday_night_wake`, `release_to_bed_minutes`), Scenario |\n| `opportunity.py` | Spec steps 1–8; conflicts; comparison with changed, removed, added, and fixed-event changes |\n| `weekend.py` | Kickoff bands, `ClockTime`, templates, `build_weekend`, the sleep window rule, `evaluate_weekend` / `compare_weekends`, flags |\n| `adapter.py` | Forecast seam; the only adapter returns a status, never a number |\n| `persistence.py` | Scenario JSON round-trip; game-weekend files (inputs only, rebuilt on load) |\n| `itinerary_csv.py` | Staff CSV in and out; import validation; anchoring from day + time form |\n| `cli.py` | `python -m sleep_scheduler <baseline> <candidate>` (each `.csv` or `.json`) |\n\n## Design confirmed (2026-09-15)\n\nThe full answer table is in `SLEEP-SCHEDULER-SPEC.md` under \"Release 1 design\ndecisions\". In short: Release 1 compares Baylor football game weekends\n(Thursday evening to Sunday midday) for **Friday practice/meeting times against\nSaturday kickoff** and **road-game departures, including the late return**. It\nreports sleep opportunity per night for one group (the travel party, or the\nwhole roster at home). Applied Performance runs it locally: CSV in, one-page\nHTML comparison out. There is no Sunday lift (player off day), and subgroups\nare out of Release 1.\n\n## Next\n\n1. **Real baselines are on Drive (2026-09-16).** Eric verified the extracted\n   times; `game1-auburn-baseline.csv` and `game2-pvamu-baseline.csv` sit next to\n   the staff PDFs in the GAME_WEEK_ITINERARIES\\2026 folder and run cleanly.\n   Entry conventions used there: check-ins as 10-minute items ending at the\n   \"by\" time; \"available\", some-players, and who-unclear items `optional`;\n   missing ends run to the next item (daytime only); Saturday released by the\n   facility bus (road) or post-game (home).\n2. **First real candidate rejected (2026-09-16).** Against 9h the road Saturday\n   night was the only night short, by less than half an hour. The candidate cut\n   post-game to leave earlier; Eric ruled post-game fixed (media obligations,\n   players changing, equipment collection). The CSV and page on Drive are\n   titled REJECTED. With post-game fixed, the road return after an afternoon\n   kickoff has no lever left inside the model except the travel legs\n   themselves (airport transfer, landing-to-facility bus). The shortfall is\n   smaller than the uncertainty in the 60-min release assumption, so treat\n   that night as at target, not a problem to fix.\n3. **Watch for the cases the model is for:** a later road kickoff (return\n   after midnight), an early kickoff (released-night floor, open question 4),\n   or Friday evening items running past curfew minus wind-down (both real\n   Fridays have zero slack).\n4. **Each new game week:** enter the itinerary CSV on Drive, run it against\n   the 9h target, and build a candidate only when a night is short.\n5. **Kickoff what-ifs (2026-09-16).** `python -m sleep_scheduler export <weekend>\n   <out.csv>` writes game-day rows as `K` offsets (refuses to overwrite). Copy\n   the export, change only `kickoff`, compare. First use, on Drive: the real\n   road trip with a hypothetical night kickoff. Saturday night falls several\n   hours short, while Friday gains only because the wake-up call follows\n   kickoff, which a real night itinerary would not do. Caveat: the Saturday\n   timeline is scaled from an afternoon itinerary. For late returns the\n   binding edge is the assumed Sunday wake. Sunday is not planned beyond the\n   medical check-in (Eric, 2026-09-16), so there is no Sunday lever either:\n   a late road kickoff is a cost to report, not a schedule to fix.\n\n- Deferred out of Release 1: forecast engine, subgroups/non-travel,\n  `SleepObservation`, candidate generation (CP-SAT), archive migration, the\n  athlete mobile itinerary, hosting and roles.\n- `AGENTS.md` still carries an unfolded `.from-windows` merge block (a stale\n  pre-interview copy); safe to delete once someone confirms nothing in it is\n  wanted.\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. (Resolved 2026-09-16: the six itinerary decisions are confirmed.)\n2. A home game with a Friday hotel uses `at=\"away\"` (game zone = home zone) to\n   mark the Friday window as a hotel window. Fine for now; revisit if a\n   template needs a hotel in the home zone on a road trip.\n3. Should the first obligation after the start ever push it (a travel leg\n   departing after curfew)? Under a curfew it is now a conflict. Real itineraries\n   put no travel after curfew, and Saturday uses release instead. Revisit only\n   if a real case appears.\n4. **Released nights have no floor.** `released_by` assumes a late release. For\n   an early home kickoff (game over ~15:00, +45m) the window line would print a\n   mid-afternoon start while the table clips it to the 18:00 reporting start\n   (~15h). Options: allow `curfew` and `released_by` together (start = the later\n   of the two), or give the early band a clock-time Saturday curfew. Decide when\n   an early-kickoff itinerary arrives; do not draft an early template before.\n5. (Resolved 2026-09-16: target 9h opportunity, 30-min wind-down, 60-min\n   release to bed, 09:00 Sunday wake. Eric deferred the call; evidence in the\n   spec under \"Assumption set for real weekends\".)\n\nStill needed from staff: verification of the extracted itinerary times, an\nearly-kickoff itinerary, and confirmation of the 30-minute wind-down buffer.\n\n## Gotchas\n\n- **Real itineraries never go in Git.** They are team schedules (AGENTS.md\n  archive boundary). Keep them and anything derived from them on Drive,\n  including times and night results quoted in STATUS or handoffs. The\n  synthetic home template copies their *shape*, never their times.\n- **Post-game is not compressible** (Eric, 2026-09-16: media, players change,\n  equipment). Enter it as a non-movable item; never build a candidate that\n  shortens it.\n- Real comparison pages and any PDFs printed from them go on Drive, never in\n  the repo or a published artifact.\n- `zoneinfo` does not refuse impossible local times; `temporal.LocalTime`\n  raises on DST gaps and ambiguous times. Do not bypass it. `ClockTime`\n  resolves through it.\n- `Interval` equality ignores zone provenance on purpose; round-trip tests\n  compare serialized dicts to check zones.\n- Events are mandatory **by default**; only `schedule.OPTIONAL_KINDS` are\n  ignored. Never reintroduce a mandatory whitelist. \"Treatment available\"-style\n  items must be entered with an optional kind.\n- Curfew and wake-up are `NightMarker`s, never Events. A marker has exactly one\n  of `curfew` or `released_by`.\n- The wind-down buffer **is** `pre_bed_minutes`; do not add a second field.\n  `release_to_bed_minutes` is different: it *replaces* the buffer at a\n  released night's start.\n- `morning_minutes` widens the block before every obligation and can clip the\n  window before a wake-up call; fixtures use 0.\n- A staff end override is settled before obligations are checked; a start\n  override is applied after.\n- Night reporting intervals are fixed by date (18:00–12:00 in the night's zone,\n  dated from the start before any push). Never derive them from the proposed\n  window.\n- Kickoff-anchored events compare by offset; a kickoff change is one\n  `kickoff_shift`. Clock events compare by instant. Change detection covers\n  start, end, removed, and added.\n- `ClockTime.day` counts from the kickoff date **on the stadium clock**.\n- Game-weekend files store inputs and are rebuilt on load; never add resolved\n  instants. Newer keys are optional, so `FORMAT_VERSION` is 1.\n- The CLI reads game-weekend files only; `--window` is gone.\n- Itinerary \"EST/CST\" in September means daylight time; use IANA zones.\n- **Editing CSVs in Excel:** prefer Save As -> \"CSV UTF-8\". Import also\n  accepts Excel's plain CSV (cp1252) and its `10:45:00` / `10:45 AM` time\n  rewrites, but check that times survived and that `K-4:15` cells were not\n  turned into dates or formulas.\n- The `game` row must start at the `kickoff` setting; import refuses a\n  mismatch rather than shifting every clock-typed Saturday row.\n- The synthetic fixtures were generated once from `tests/synthetic.py`; the\n  generator was not kept. Edit the JSON directly.\n\n--- HANDOFF\n# Handoff: itinerary-shaped model and staff CSV input, 2026-09-16\n\n## What was worked on and why\n\nPicked up from `2026-09-15-game-weekend-model.md`. The first real staff itineraries (Auburn, PVAMU) showed the template model did not fit them. Curfew and Friday practice/meetings are clock times regardless of kickoff. A breakfast window erased Friday night. Saturday has no curfew. The session reshaped the model, got the user's confirmation of six itinerary decisions, and built Package 2 (staff CSV input), all test-first. 133 → 194 tests.\n\n## Decisions made (with reasoning)\n\n- **Six itinerary decisions, confirmed by the user 2026-09-16** (spec table \"Decisions from the first real itineraries\"):\n  1. Friday curfew is a clock time.\n  2. Friday practice and meetings are clock times; kickoff drives Saturday.\n  3. Breakfast counts as its check-in; \"available\" items are optional.\n  4. Saturday has no curfew and starts 45 minutes after release.\n  5. There is no Thursday night until an itinerary gives a curfew.\n  6. Neutral-site games are road games.\n\n  The spec's Q5 and Q6 rows were amended to match.\n- **Window start rule:** only an obligation that begins before the current start pushes it. Later ones stay inside the window as conflicts. This fixed the breakfast erasure (on the real road itinerary, Friday went from no window to a clipped window with a visible conflict, or a full window set by curfew with breakfast entered as its check-in). It also closed the old open question 7.\n- **Released nights:** `NightMarker(curfew=None, released_by=id)` plus `AssumptionSet.release_to_bed_minutes`. It replaces the wind-down buffer at that edge rather than adding to it, and it is labelled as an assumption in the report.\n- **Clock anchoring:** `ClockTime(day, HH:MM)`. Clock entries carry no kickoff anchor, so a kickoff change doesn't report Friday events as moved. An entry must place both edges the same way, and overrides must match the entry's anchoring, so an entry can't be quietly re-anchored.\n- **CSV anchoring has no column.** A `K` offset follows kickoff. A clock time before game day stays on the clock. A clock time on game day or later becomes an offset from the file's kickoff. This encodes decision 2 directly.\n- **Import refuses unknown kinds.** The model deliberately treats unknown kinds as mandatory, so a typo would silently block sleep.\n- **The game row must start at `kickoff`.** Otherwise a typo shifts every clock-typed Saturday row without warning.\n- **Export writes game-day rows as `K` offsets** so a copy re-flows when kickoff changes. It refuses window overrides and extra events rather than writing them lossily.\n- **Real itinerary data stays on Drive.** `itineraries/` is gitignored, and all fixtures use invented times.\n\n## Files created or modified\n\nRepo `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\sleep-scheduler\\.claude\\worktrees\\calendar-core`, branch `worktree-calendar-core`, draft PR #1:\n\n- `src/sleep_scheduler/weekend.py`, `schedule.py`, `persistence.py`, `cli.py`\n- `src/sleep_scheduler/itinerary_csv.py` (new)\n- `tests/test_clock_anchoring.py`, `tests/test_itinerary_csv.py` (new); `tests/synthetic.py`, `test_windows.py`, `test_weekend_compare.py`, `test_weekend_file.py`, `test_cli.py`\n- `fixtures/home-night-hotel-baseline.csv`, `home-night-hotel-candidate.csv`, `road-afternoon-baseline.csv` (new, synthetic)\n- `SLEEP-SCHEDULER-SPEC.md` (window rule, confirmed decisions, \"Staff CSV format\"), `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `docs/STATUS.md`, `.gitignore`\n- `docs/handoffs/2026-09-16-itinerary-model-and-csv-input.md` (this file)\n\nOutside the repo: `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` (Sleep scheduler thread). Nothing was written to Drive this session.\n\n## Later the same day: real weekends, target, HTML page, export\n\n- **Eric verified the extracted times.** Real baselines were entered as CSVs **on Drive only** (`G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\99_UNSORTED\\From_Desktop\\TECHNOLOGY\\FAST\\GAME_WEEK_ITINERARIES\\2026\\`): `game1-auburn-baseline.csv` and `game2-pvamu-baseline.csv`. Entry conventions are in STATUS Next 1.\n- **Assumption set decided (Eric deferred the call):** 9h opportunity target, 30-min wind-down, 60-min release to bed, 09:00 Sunday wake. Reasoning: athlete sleep efficiency is about 81%, so 8h in bed is about 6.5h asleep. Evidence is in the spec under \"Assumption set for real weekends\".\n- **Package 3 built:** `src/sleep_scheduler/report_html.py`, `tests/test_report_html.py`, and a `--html PATH` CLI option. The page is self-contained and prints to one Letter page (checked with headless Chrome).\n- **First real candidate rejected.** It moved the road post-game departure earlier; Eric ruled post-game fixed (media obligations, players changing, equipment collection). Q7 in the spec is amended, and the Drive files are titled REJECTED. The remaining road Saturday shortfall is within the uncertainty of the release assumption.\n- **Export command:** `python -m sleep_scheduler export <weekend> <out.csv>` (in `cli.py`, tests in `tests/test_cli.py`). First use, on Drive and marked HYPOTHETICAL: the road trip with a night kickoff. Saturday night falls several hours short. After a late return, the binding edge is the assumed Sunday wake, so the levers are on Sunday (no morning obligations, or flying home Sunday).\n- **Correction:** real times had been quoted in STATUS, the spec, and this handoff. They were replaced with generic wording (`a04882a`). Earlier commits still contain them in the private repo's history; removing them would need a force-push, which was not done.\n\nAdditional repo files this session: `src/sleep_scheduler/report_html.py`, `tests/test_report_html.py`, `README.md`. Drive files: the two baselines, `game1-auburn-baseline-kickoff-relative.csv`, `game1-auburn-candidate-depart-30-earlier.csv` (REJECTED), `game1-auburn-comparison-depart-30-earlier.html` (REJECTED), `game1-auburn-whatif-1930-kickoff.csv`, and `game1-auburn-whatif-1930-kickoff-comparison.html`.\n\n## Next actions\n\n1. When a night-kickoff road game or an early-kickoff itinerary arrives, enter it on Drive and run it. Those are the cases the tool is for.\n2. (Closed: Eric does not plan Sunday beyond the medical check-in, so Sunday options are not pursued.)\n3. Each game week: enter the itinerary CSV, run it against 9h, and build a candidate only when a night is short and a real lever exists.\n\n## Mid-flight\n\n- Released nights have no earliest start; an early home kickoff would start Saturday mid-afternoon. Decide when an early-kickoff itinerary arrives (STATUS open question 4).\n- PR #1 merged to `main` at Eric's request (2026-09-16).\n- Old commits contain real itinerary times (private repo). Eric decided to keep them; no history rewrite.\n2026-09-16 18:31:41 -0500 Eric Rash Merge pull request #1 from erash11/worktree-calendar-core\n2026-09-16 18:31:24 -0500 Eric Rash Merge remote-tracking branch 'origin/main' into worktree-calendar-core\n2026-09-16 18:30:58 -0500 Eric Rash Record Sunday (medical check-in only) and history decisions; mark PR #1 merged\n2026-09-16 18:25:06 -0500 Eric Rash Closeout 2026-09-16 (later): handoff and STATUS for real weekends, HTML page, export\n2026-09-16 18:01:54 -0500 Eric Rash CLI export command for kickoff-slot what-ifs\n2026-09-16 17:57:46 -0500 Eric Rash Record post-game as fixed; first real candidate rejected","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"65069d8a-bfac-45d8-b285-1013b13df43e","session_id":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/sleep-scheduler","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
