ssistant recommendations generally, are not approved product decisions unless the user confirms them.** The calendar core and opportunity calculation are implemented (branch `worktree-calendar-core`, see `docs/STATUS.md`). No forecasting engine is selected and no physiological model is validated for Baylor in this project.\n---\n> **Do not treat the proposed specification or assistant recommendations as approved product decisions.** No application has been built, no forecasting engine selected, and no physiological model validated for Baylor in this project.\n72c72\n< ## Design interview: completed 2026-09-15\n---\n> ## Paused design interview: resume here\n74c74\n< The user invoked `mattpocock-skills:grill-me` (routes to `grilling`). Four rounds, 19 questions, were answered and the resulting design was confirmed by the user. The authoritative record is the \"Release 1 design decisions\" table in `SLEEP-SCHEDULER-SPEC.md`. Summary:\n---\n> The user invoked `mattpocock-skills:grill-me`. Its skill routes to `grilling`: interview in dependency-aware rounds, ask the currently answerable questions with recommendations, and wait for answers. Do not start implementation on the interview's behalf before the user confirms shared understanding. The user can explicitly change this workflow or authorize different work.\n76,81c76\n< - **Decisions supported:** Friday practice and meeting times against Saturday kickoff, and road-game departures including the late return. First working example: Friday times before an early road kickoff.\n< - **Scope:** one Baylor football game weekend per scenario (Thursday evening to Sunday midday), road games and home games with a team hotel. One group: the travel party, or the full roster at home. No subgroups or individual athlete data.\n< - **Output:** sleep opportunity per night against one editable target. No effectiveness engine yet; one is expected later, and the product is not a FAST replacement until then.\n< - **Timeline:** game-related events are offsets from kickoff, drawn from three kickoff-band templates (early before 12:00, afternoon 12:00-17:59, night 18:00+). Movable levers: departures, Friday practice, meetings. Kickoff is fixed.\n< - **Sleep window:** start = later of curfew and last obligation end + wind-down buffer; end = wake-up call. Sunday is a player off day (no Sunday lift; lifts are Monday and Wednesday), so Saturday night ends at an editable assumed wake time labelled as an assumption.\n< - **Operation:** Applied Performance builds and interprets; coaches and operations review a one-page comparison and decide. CSV template in, printable HTML out, run locally with no hosting.\n---\n> The first round has **no answers yet**:\n83c78,81\n< Still unsettled and deliberately deferred: forecast engine, hosting and roles, subgroups, candidate generation. Neutral-site games count as road games (confirmed 2026-09-16). Real template offsets, the Saturday assumed wake time, and the wind-down default are staff-supplied data, not open decisions. Recompute with a new interview round before building anything outside this scope.\n---\n> 1. **First real decision:** What specific scheduling decision should the app improve? Assistant recommendation: compare football game-trip travel and next-morning schedules while preserving required activities.\n> 2. **Required forecasting:** Is comparing sleep opportunity useful in the first version, or is the FAST-style mental-effectiveness curve essential? Assistant recommendation: prototype the workflow first, with a credible engine required before calling the product a FAST replacement.\n> 3. **Who is modeled:** A representative athlete, profiles/subgroups, or individuals? Assistant recommendation: begin with a few staff-defined sleep profiles applied to a team schedule.\n> 4. **Who operates it:** Who creates, interprets, and approves scenarios? Assistant recommendation: Applied Performance creates/interprets; operations and coaches review tradeoffs; athletes initially receive the itinerary.\n85c83\n< The ask-matt routing recommendation was grill-me -> prototype -> specification -> tickets -> implementation. This is a suggested route, not authorization to execute every stage or to create other tasks.\n---\n> Audience is already settled: **Baylor athletes and team travel**. Do not ask that again. First sport/workflow, forecast requirements, modeling unit, users/authority, engine, hosting, and deployment remain unsettled. Recompute subsequent interview questions after answers; avoid asking downstream implementation questions prematurely.\n86a85,86\n> The ask-matt routing recommendation was grill-me → prototype → specification → tickets → implementation. This is a suggested route, not authorization to execute every stage or to create other tasks.\n> \n103d102\n< \n108,110d106\n< \n< ---\n< \n\n- Start: run `git pull`, then read docs/STATUS.md before doing anything else.\n- End: update docs/STATUS.md (done, next, open questions), commit, and `git push`.\n- Never leave uncommitted work at the end of a session.\n\n---\n\n## Merged from windows copy (2026-09-11)\n\n<!-- Contents of AGENTS.md.from-windows, kept verbatim so nothing is lost. Fold into the sections above when convenient. -->","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"eace8634-7c17-4d96-bb72-b2d4eaa2021a","timestamp":"2026-09-17T00:14:38.543Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"3c3\n< Last updated: September 15, 2026.\n---\n> Last updated: September 9, 2026.\n9c9\n< Original and recovered materials remain in `G:\\My Drive\\00_Primary_Storage_Drive\\99_UNSORTED\\From_Desktop\\TECHNOLOGY\\FAST`. Archive-relative paths below describe that external archive, not files promised in this repository. See README.md for the boundary. Track authored documentation, scripts, and future code/synthetic tests only; exclude source schedules, installation media, extracted binaries, and bundled manuals/schema.\n---\n> Original and recovered materials remain in `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\OneDrive - Baylor Univers