{"parentUuid":"f568fe75-a6ba-4899-a5ec-8fa72ddd5adf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf83kyw7sJryJ71bYxRX3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QrdeZF7QeeNEXJw7h6a913","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat ~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js; cat ~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md | sed -n 1,60p","description":"Read the hook source and the timeout-cap memory"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":528,"cache_read_input_tokens":107840,"output_tokens":162,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":528,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":162,"cache_read_input_tokens":107840,"cache_creation_input_tokens":528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":528},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01QrdeZF7QeeNEXJw7h6a913":{"command":"cat ~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js; cat ~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md | sed -n 1,60p","description":"Read the hook source and the timeout-cap memory"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cf83kyWovUPCDHG7gjGJB","type":"assistant","uuid":"02f49de8-71d4-4aff-a438-3cff73adab28","timestamp":"2026-09-17T00:15:46.924Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/sleep-scheduler","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"02f49de8-71d4-4aff-a438-3cff73adab28","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d244362e-5606-4d0d-bb3a-655bd0a811f4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QrdeZF7QeeNEXJw7h6a913","type":"tool_result","content":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// SessionEnd hook: re-index memsearch so transcripts written this session\n// become searchable.\n//\n// Runs the index in a DETACHED BACKGROUND WORKER (see the self-respawn block\n// below). The hook process returns in milliseconds; the worker outlives it and\n// indexes at its own pace.\n//\n// This replaced a synchronous spawnSync capped at 45s, changed 2026-09-02.\n// The old design assumed a ~17s fixed floor and a \"~5s incremental\" — numbers\n// that were true when they were written and are not any more. Measured on this\n// Mac 2026-09-02 against the current corpus (10 paths, ~1,450 files, of which\n// SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings is 593 and SecondBrain/Knowledge is 334):\n//   * no-op run, nothing new to embed ....... 33s   (was ~17s)\n//   * one day of accumulated content ....... 278s   (232 chunks, ~1s/chunk)\n// So the 45s cap left ~12s of embedding budget — under a dozen new chunks.\n// Every ordinary session-end index was timing out, and incremental indexing had\n// quietly degraded to \"whatever the 09:00 nightly full reindex picks up\",\n// leaving search up to 24h stale. The tell was six session-ends inside 47\n// minutes on 2026-09-01 producing a 7-long ETIMEDOUT streak. Raising the cap\n// was not an option: a correct cap is minutes, and a SessionEnd hook may not\n// block session teardown for minutes.\n//\n// The original rationale for staying synchronous was a WINDOWS process-group\n// race — a detached child got killed when the parent hard-exited before its\n// group was established. **That rationale is dead.** This machine has been\n// macOS since 2026-08-05, where detached + unref is reliable. Do not \"restore\"\n// the synchronous version on the strength of that comment.\n//\n// The worker keeps the full original body: it owns the PID lock (so clustered\n// session-ends still elect a single indexer), writes INDEX_LOG, and runs every\n// error-classification branch. Its spawnSync cap is now 600s — no longer a\n// budget, just a runaway guard, so an ETIMEDOUT genuinely means \">10 minutes\"\n// and is worth escalating. See project_memsearch_setup.md.\n\nconst { spawnSync, spawn } = require('child_process');\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst os = require('os');\n\n// --- Detach: re-exec ourselves as a background worker, then return at once. ---\n// stdio MUST be 'ignore'. Inheriting or piping keeps the harness's pipe open,\n// so it would wait for the child anyway and we would have rebuilt the timeout\n// with extra steps. The lock below is then taken by the WORKER's pid, which is\n// what isProcessAlive() needs in order to coordinate late-arriving sessions.\nif (!process.argv.includes('--worker')) {\n  try {\n    spawn(process.execPath, [__filename, '--worker'], {\n      detached: true,\n      stdio: 'ignore',\n    }).unref();\n  } catch (_) {}\n  process.exit(0);\n}\n\n// Declared before resolvePaths() runs: `const PATHS = resolvePaths()` executes\n// below, and resolvePaths now calls logErr on the fallback branch. If ERR_LOG\n// were still declared further down it would be in the temporal dead zone at\n// that moment, and logErr's own try/catch would swallow the ReferenceError —\n// turning the loud fallback warning silent again.\nconst ERR_LOG = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.errors.log');\n\nfunction logErr(msg) {\n  try { fs.appendFileSync(ERR_LOG, `[${new Date().toISOString()}] memsearch-sync: ${msg}\\n`); }\n  catch (_) {}\n}\n\n// Single source of truth, shared with daily-backup.ps1 and the Codex hook.\n// Was ~/Desktop/DEV on Windows; DEV lives at ~/DEV on the Mac. The stale path\n// silently resolved to the fallback below for months after the migration, so\n// SecondBrain/Knowledge, the Mac memory slug, morning-briefings, KeystoneOS\n// decisions and .codex/memories were never indexed by this hook. Fixed\n// 2026-08-14. A fallback now also writes to .errors.log — see resolvePaths.\n// 2026-09-10: DEV lives at ~/DEV on the Mac and ~/Desktop/DEV on the Windows\n// laptop (both machines active again). Resolve whichever exists so neither\n// machine silently falls back. Paths in the config may use {DEV} as well as {HOME}.\nconst DEV_ROOT = [\n  path.join(os.homedir(), 'DEV'),\n  path.join(os.homedir(), 'Desktop', 'DEV'),\n].find(p => { try { return fs.statSync(path.join(p, 'KeystoneOS')).isDirectory(); } catch (_) { return false; } })\n  || path.join(os.homedir(), 'DEV');\nconst KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG = path.join(\n  DEV_ROOT, 'KeystoneOS', 'data', 'memory-index-paths.json'\n);\n\nfunction resolvePaths() {\n  // Mac-correct emergency set, used only when the config above is unreadable\n  // or malformed. Kept deliberately small: these are the paths whose loss would\n  // be felt first. The legacy C--Users-eric-rash slug is read-only Windows\n  // history — indexing it is harmless and keeps that recall working.\n  const fallback = [\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'projects', '-Users-kristinarash-DEV', 'memory'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'projects', 'C--Users-eric-rash', 'memory'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'claude-memory-compiler', 'daily'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'claude-memory-compiler', 'knowledge'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), 'SecondBrain', 'Knowledge'),\n  ];\n  try {\n    const raw = fs.readFileSync(KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG, 'utf8');\n    const parsed = JSON.parse(raw);\n    if (!Array.isArray(parsed.paths) || parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n      logErr(`index config ${KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG} has no usable paths — using built-in fallback (${fallback.length} paths)`);\n      return fallback;\n    }\n    return parsed.paths.map(p => p.replace('{DEV}', DEV_ROOT).replace('{HOME}', os.homedir()).split('/').join(path.sep));\n  } catch (e) {\n    // Loud, not silent: a silent fallback is exactly how the Desktop/DEV drift\n    // went unnoticed from the Windows migration until 2026-08-14.\n    logErr(`index config unreadable (${KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG}): ${(e && e.message ? e.message : String(e)).slice(0, 160)} — using built-in fallback (${fallback.length} paths)`);\n    return fallback;\n  }\n}\n\nconst PATHS = resolvePaths();\n// Truncated each run, so it always shows the most recent index result and\n// never grows. Check this if recent sessions aren't turning up in search.\nconst INDEX_LOG = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.memsearch-index.log');\n// When several sessions end in a cluster, each spawnSync below would otherwise\n// block the full timeout waiting on memsearch's own lock and then time out. The\n// lock is acquired ATOMICALLY (open 'wx') so only one winner indexes per cluster;\n// late arrivals see EEXIST and skip immediately — that run covers their new\n// content too. The owner PID is checked before a lock is reclaimed. Age is only\n// a fallback for locks written by an older version that did not record a PID.\nconst LOCK_FILE = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.memsearch-index.lock');\nconst LOCK_STALE_MS = 5 * 60 * 1000;\n// A single ETIMEDOUT is self-healing (the next session's incremental index\n// catches up) and usually just means session-end contention — three fired on\n// 2026-07-08 alone while the index stayed healthy. Only escalate to .errors.log\n// after this many CONSECUTIVE timeouts; the count lives in a state file and any\n// clean run resets it. Non-timeout failures still escalate immediately.\nconst TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.memsearch-timeout-streak');\n// Raised 3 -> 6 on 2026-07-24: the nightly KeystoneOS-DailyBackup task now runs\n// an uncapped full reindex and resets this counter, so heavy-day backlogs are\n// guaranteed to clear overnight. Alarm only if even the nightly isn't keeping up.\nconst TIMEOUT_STREAK_THRESHOLD = 6;\n// Signatures that mean \"another writer holds milvus.db/LOCK\", not \"the database\n// is damaged\". Deliberately narrow: matched against the flock/lock-acquisition\n// text, NOT against milvus-lite's wording \"may not be compatible with Milvus\n// Lite 3.x\" — that same sentence is emitted for genuine incompatibility too, so\n// matching it would mask real corruption as transient. A truly broken database\n// produces none of these and still escalates immediately.\nconst LOCK_BUSY_RE = /DataDirLockedError|another process holds the lock|Resource temporarily unavailable|_acquire_lock|fcntl\\.flock/i;\n\nconst existing = PATHS.filter(p => fs.existsSync(p));\nif (existing.length === 0) process.exit(0);\n\nfunction isProcessAlive(pid) {\n  if (!Number.isInteger(pid) || pid <= 0) return false;\n  try {\n    process.kill(pid, 0);\n    return true;\n  } catch (e) {\n    return e && e.code === 'EPERM';\n  }\n}\n\n// --- Acquire the lock atomically. Only one process wins per cluster. ---\nlet haveLock = false;\ntry {\n  const fd = fs.openSync(LOCK_FILE, 'wx'); // fails if the file already exists\n  fs.writeSync(fd, String(process.pid));\n  fs.closeSync(fd);\n  haveLock = true;\n} catch (e) {\n  // Lock exists. If its owner is alive, skip regardless of age. Only reclaim a\n  // dead owner (or an old lock with no readable owner PID).\n  let ownerPid = null;\n  try {\n    ownerPid = Number.parseInt(fs.readFileSync(LOCK_FILE, 'utf8').trim(), 10);\n  } catch (_) {}\n  if (isProcessAlive(ownerPid)) process.exit(0);\n\n  let stale = false;\n  try { stale = (Date.now() - fs.statSync(LOCK_FILE).mtimeMs) >= LOCK_STALE_MS; }\n  catch (_) { stale = true; } // lock vanished between open and stat — race; treat as reclaimable\n  if (!stale) process.exit(0);\n  try {\n    fs.unlinkSync(LOCK_FILE);\n    const fd = fs.openSync(LOCK_FILE, 'wx');\n    fs.writeSync(fd, String(process.pid));\n    fs.closeSync(fd);\n    haveLock = true;\n  }\n  catch (_) { process.exit(0); }\n}\n\nfunction releaseLock() {\n  if (haveLock) { try { fs.unlinkSync(LOCK_FILE); } catch (_) {} haveLock = false; }\n}\n\ntry {\n  const res = spawnSync('memsearch', ['index', ...existing], {\n    // Avoid an intermediate cmd.exe. On timeout, the direct child is killed\n    // instead of leaving an orphaned Python indexer behind.\n    shell: false,\n    windowsHide: true,\n    encoding: 'utf8',\n    // Runaway guard, not a budget. We are detached, so nothing is waiting on\n    // this. A full day's backlog measured 278s on 2026-09-02, so 600s means a\n    // timeout here is a genuine hang worth escalating - not normal work being\n    // cut off, which is what the old 45s cap was doing on every session end.\n    timeout: 600000,\n  });\n\n  const out = `${res.stdout || ''}${res.stderr || ''}`.trim();\n  try { fs.writeFileSync(INDEX_LOG, out ? out + '\\n' : '(no output)\\n'); } catch (_) {}\n\n  // Transient, self-healing failures (the next incremental index catches up)\n  // escalate to .errors.log only after a consecutive streak. Two classes:\n  // ETIMEDOUT (session-end contention) and Milvus-unreachable (Docker Desktop\n  // hasn't started yet — morning scheduled sessions routinely end before the\n  // Milvus containers are up; observed 2026-07-16 07:40, self-healed by 08:43).\n  function bumpTransientStreak(label, detail) {\n    let streak = 0;\n    try { streak = parseInt(fs.readFileSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE, 'utf8'), 10) || 0; } catch (_) {}\n    streak += 1;\n    try { fs.writeFileSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE, String(streak)); } catch (_) {}\n    if (streak >= TIMEOUT_STREAK_THRESHOLD) {\n      logErr(`index ${label} ${streak}x consecutively — indexing may not be keeping up: ${detail.slice(0, 200)}`);\n    } else {\n      try { fs.appendFileSync(INDEX_LOG, `(${label} — streak ${streak}/${TIMEOUT_STREAK_THRESHOLD}, next session catches up)\\n`); } catch (_) {}\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (res.error && res.error.code === 'ETIMEDOUT') {\n    bumpTransientStreak('ETIMEDOUT', res.error.message);\n  } else if (res.error) {\n    logErr(`index ${res.error.code || 'error'}: ${res.error.message.slice(0, 300)}`);\n    try { fs.unlinkSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE); } catch (_) {}\n  } else if (res.status !== 0 && /Fail connecting to server/i.test(out)) {\n    bumpTransientStreak('Milvus unreachable', (res.stderr || out));\n  } else if (res.status !== 0 && LOCK_BUSY_RE.test(out)) {\n    // Another writer (nightly reindex, a second session, a manual index) holds\n    // milvus.db/LOCK. Transient by definition — the next incremental run covers\n    // this session's content. Previously this fell through to the hard-error\n    // branch and spammed .errors.log; worse, milvus-lite words the lock error\n    // as \"may not be compatible with Milvus Lite 3.x\", which has already caused\n    // one destructive rebuild. Never treat this as corruption.\n    bumpTransientStreak('index busy (lock held)', (res.stderr || out));\n  } else if (res.status !== 0) {\n    logErr(`index exit ${res.status}: ${(res.stderr || '').slice(0, 300)}`);\n    try { fs.unlinkSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE); } catch (_) {}\n  } else {\n    // Clean run — reset the consecutive-timeout streak.\n    try { fs.unlinkSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE); } catch (_) {}\n  }\n} finally {\n  releaseLock();\n}\n\nprocess.exit(0);\n---\nname: memsearch-hook-timeout-cap\ndescription: \"the SessionEnd memsearch hook's fixed 45s cap was outrun by corpus growth, so every session-end index timed out and incremental recall silently degraded to the 09:00 nightly; fixed 2026-09-02 by detaching the indexer\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-09-02T12:33:29.526Z\n  originSessionId: 00fe7fc2-f53c-471d-b407-c777e8cc679c\n---\n\n`~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` (SessionEnd) ran `memsearch index <10 paths>` through\n`spawnSync` with a **45s cap**, sized in July against a \"~17s fixed floor, ~5s incremental\"\nmeasurement. Measured again 2026-09-02 against the current corpus (~1,450 files;\n`SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings` alone is 593 and `SecondBrain/Knowledge` 334):\n\n| run | time |\n|---|---|\n| no-op, nothing new to embed | **33s** (was ~17s) |\n| one day of accumulated content | **278s** (232 chunks, ~1s/chunk) |\n\nSo the cap left ~12s of embedding budget — under a dozen new chunks. **Every ordinary\nsession-end index was timing out.** Incremental indexing had quietly degraded to \"whatever\nthe 09:00 nightly full reindex picks up\", leaving search up to 24h stale.\n\n**Why it stayed invisible for weeks.** Three dampers stacked:\n`daily-backup.ps1` resets `.memsearch-timeout-streak` every morning, the escalation\nthreshold is 6 consecutive timeouts, and the hook writes only to `.memsearch-index.log`\nbelow that. So a normal day of 2-4 session-ends never reached the threshold and never\nsurfaced. It took **six session-ends inside 47 minutes** (2026-09-01, 21:09-21:56) to\nproduce a 7-long streak and one `.errors.log` line. The alarm fired on burst *frequency*,\nnever on the underlying constant failure.\n\n**The misread that line invites:** ETIMEDOUT + a clustered burst pattern-matches\n[[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]] (session-end lock contention), and the hook's own comments\nsay exactly that. It was not contention — `pgrep`/`lsof` showed no holder and the DB was\nhealthy. **Time the actual command before believing the comment.**\n\n**Fix (2026-09-02): detached background worker.** The hook now re-execs itself as\n`node memsearch-sync.js --worker` with `detached: true, stdio: 'ignore'` and `.unref()`,\nthen exits — measured **42ms**. The worker keeps the entire original body, so it owns the\nPID lock (clustered session-ends still elect one indexer), writes `INDEX_LOG`, and runs\nevery error branch. Its `spawnSync` cap is now 600s: a runaway guard, not a budget, so an\nETIMEDOUT there genuinely means \">10 minutes\".\n\n- `stdio: 'ignore'` is **not optional** — inherited or piped stdio keeps the harness's pipe\n  open and it waits for the child anyway, rebuilding the timeout with extra steps.\n- Self-respawn, **not** an `sh -c` wrapper: the wrapper would discard the lock ownership\n  and all four error-classification branches.\n- The old header justified staying synchronous with a **Windows** process-group race.\n  That rationale died with the 2026-08-05 migration; the comment now says so, because the\n  next reader would otherwise \"restore\" the bug.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- A fixed timeout sized against a measured corpus is a **dated** number. When the corpus is\n  something that grows every day (the vault, meetings, dailies), the cap has an expiry date\n  nobody writes down. Prefer detaching over raising a cap; if a cap must stay, record the\n  measurement and the date beside it so drift is visible.\n- Never size a SessionEnd hook to block. Session teardown cannot wait minutes, so any\n  design where \"correct\" means minutes must be asynchronous.\n- Both harnesses wire their own copy. `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` is now a","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"144b35cf-4a57-4b35-b440-d6e60856fd9b","timestamp":"2026-09-17T00:15:49.002Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// SessionEnd hook: re-index memsearch so transcripts written this session\n// become searchable.\n//\n// Runs the index in a DETACHED BACKGROUND WORKER (see the self-respawn block\n// below). The hook process returns in milliseconds; the worker outlives it and\n// indexes at its own pace.\n//\n// This replaced a synchronous spawnSync capped at 45s, changed 2026-09-02.\n// The old design assumed a ~17s fixed floor and a \"~5s incremental\" — numbers\n// that were true when they were written and are not any more. Measured on this\n// Mac 2026-09-02 against the current corpus (10 paths, ~1,450 files, of which\n// SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings is 593 and SecondBrain/Knowledge is 334):\n//   * no-op run, nothing new to embed ....... 33s   (was ~17s)\n//   * one day of accumulated content ....... 278s   (232 chunks, ~1s/chunk)\n// So the 45s cap left ~12s of embedding budget — under a dozen new chunks.\n// Every ordinary session-end index was timing out, and incremental indexing had\n// quietly degraded to \"whatever the 09:00 nightly full reindex picks up\",\n// leaving search up to 24h stale. The tell was six session-ends inside 47\n// minutes on 2026-09-01 producing a 7-long ETIMEDOUT streak. Raising the cap\n// was not an option: a correct cap is minutes, and a SessionEnd hook may not\n// block session teardown for minutes.\n//\n// The original rationale for staying synchronous was a WINDOWS process-group\n// race — a detached child got killed when the parent hard-exited before its\n// group was established. **That rationale is dead.** This machine has been\n// macOS since 2026-08-05, where detached + unref is reliable. Do not \"restore\"\n// the synchronous version on the strength of that comment.\n//\n// The worker keeps the full original body: it owns the PID lock (so clustered\n// session-ends still elect a single indexer), writes INDEX_LOG, and runs every\n// error-classification branch. Its spawnSync cap is now 600s — no longer a\n// budget, just a runaway guard, so an ETIMEDOUT genuinely means \">10 minutes\"\n// and is worth escalating. See project_memsearch_setup.md.\n\nconst { spawnSync, spawn } = require('child_process');\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst path = require('path');\nconst os = require('os');\n\n// --- Detach: re-exec ourselves as a background worker, then return at once. ---\n// stdio MUST be 'ignore'. Inheriting or piping keeps the harness's pipe open,\n// so it would wait for the child anyway and we would have rebuilt the timeout\n// with extra steps. The lock below is then taken by the WORKER's pid, which is\n// what isProcessAlive() needs in order to coordinate late-arriving sessions.\nif (!process.argv.includes('--worker')) {\n  try {\n    spawn(process.execPath, [__filename, '--worker'], {\n      detached: true,\n      stdio: 'ignore',\n    }).unref();\n  } catch (_) {}\n  process.exit(0);\n}\n\n// Declared before resolvePaths() runs: `const PATHS = resolvePaths()` executes\n// below, and resolvePaths now calls logErr on the fallback branch. If ERR_LOG\n// were still declared further down it would be in the temporal dead zone at\n// that moment, and logErr's own try/catch would swallow the ReferenceError —\n// turning the loud fallback warning silent again.\nconst ERR_LOG = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.errors.log');\n\nfunction logErr(msg) {\n  try { fs.appendFileSync(ERR_LOG, `[${new Date().toISOString()}] memsearch-sync: ${msg}\\n`); }\n  catch (_) {}\n}\n\n// Single source of truth, shared with daily-backup.ps1 and the Codex hook.\n// Was ~/Desktop/DEV on Windows; DEV lives at ~/DEV on the Mac. The stale path\n// silently resolved to the fallback below for months after the migration, so\n// SecondBrain/Knowledge, the Mac memory slug, morning-briefings, KeystoneOS\n// decisions and .codex/memories were never indexed by this hook. Fixed\n// 2026-08-14. A fallback now also writes to .errors.log — see resolvePaths.\n// 2026-09-10: DEV lives at ~/DEV on the Mac and ~/Desktop/DEV on the Windows\n// laptop (both machines active again). Resolve whichever exists so neither\n// machine silently falls back. Paths in the config may use {DEV} as well as {HOME}.\nconst DEV_ROOT = [\n  path.join(os.homedir(), 'DEV'),\n  path.join(os.homedir(), 'Desktop', 'DEV'),\n].find(p => { try { return fs.statSync(path.join(p, 'KeystoneOS')).isDirectory(); } catch (_) { return false; } })\n  || path.join(os.homedir(), 'DEV');\nconst KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG = path.join(\n  DEV_ROOT, 'KeystoneOS', 'data', 'memory-index-paths.json'\n);\n\nfunction resolvePaths() {\n  // Mac-correct emergency set, used only when the config above is unreadable\n  // or malformed. Kept deliberately small: these are the paths whose loss would\n  // be felt first. The legacy C--Users-eric-rash slug is read-only Windows\n  // history — indexing it is harmless and keeps that recall working.\n  const fallback = [\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'projects', '-Users-kristinarash-DEV', 'memory'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'projects', 'C--Users-eric-rash', 'memory'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'claude-memory-compiler', 'daily'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'claude-memory-compiler', 'knowledge'),\n    path.join(os.homedir(), 'SecondBrain', 'Knowledge'),\n  ];\n  try {\n    const raw = fs.readFileSync(KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG, 'utf8');\n    const parsed = JSON.parse(raw);\n    if (!Array.isArray(parsed.paths) || parsed.paths.length === 0) {\n      logErr(`index config ${KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG} has no usable paths — using built-in fallback (${fallback.length} paths)`);\n      return fallback;\n    }\n    return parsed.paths.map(p => p.replace('{DEV}', DEV_ROOT).replace('{HOME}', os.homedir()).split('/').join(path.sep));\n  } catch (e) {\n    // Loud, not silent: a silent fallback is exactly how the Desktop/DEV drift\n    // went unnoticed from the Windows migration until 2026-08-14.\n    logErr(`index config unreadable (${KEYSTONE_INDEX_CONFIG}): ${(e && e.message ? e.message : String(e)).slice(0, 160)} — using built-in fallback (${fallback.length} paths)`);\n    return fallback;\n  }\n}\n\nconst PATHS = resolvePaths();\n// Truncated each run, so it always shows the most recent index result and\n// never grows. Check this if recent sessions aren't turning up in search.\nconst INDEX_LOG = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.memsearch-index.log');\n// When several sessions end in a cluster, each spawnSync below would otherwise\n// block the full timeout waiting on memsearch's own lock and then time out. The\n// lock is acquired ATOMICALLY (open 'wx') so only one winner indexes per cluster;\n// late arrivals see EEXIST and skip immediately — that run covers their new\n// content too. The owner PID is checked before a lock is reclaimed. Age is only\n// a fallback for locks written by an older version that did not record a PID.\nconst LOCK_FILE = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.memsearch-index.lock');\nconst LOCK_STALE_MS = 5 * 60 * 1000;\n// A single ETIMEDOUT is self-healing (the next session's incremental index\n// catches up) and usually just means session-end contention — three fired on\n// 2026-07-08 alone while the index stayed healthy. Only escalate to .errors.log\n// after this many CONSECUTIVE timeouts; the count lives in a state file and any\n// clean run resets it. Non-timeout failures still escalate immediately.\nconst TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE = path.join(os.homedir(), '.claude', 'global-memory', 'transcripts', '.memsearch-timeout-streak');\n// Raised 3 -> 6 on 2026-07-24: the nightly KeystoneOS-DailyBackup task now runs\n// an uncapped full reindex and resets this counter, so heavy-day backlogs are\n// guaranteed to clear overnight. Alarm only if even the nightly isn't keeping up.\nconst TIMEOUT_STREAK_THRESHOLD = 6;\n// Signatures that mean \"another writer holds milvus.db/LOCK\", not \"the database\n// is damaged\". Deliberately narrow: matched against the flock/lock-acquisition\n// text, NOT against milvus-lite's wording \"may not be compatible with Milvus\n// Lite 3.x\" — that same sentence is emitted for genuine incompatibility too, so\n// matching it would mask real corruption as transient. A truly broken database\n// produces none of these and still escalates immediately.\nconst LOCK_BUSY_RE = /DataDirLockedError|another process holds the lock|Resource temporarily unavailable|_acquire_lock|fcntl\\.flock/i;\n\nconst existing = PATHS.filter(p => fs.existsSync(p));\nif (existing.length === 0) process.exit(0);\n\nfunction isProcessAlive(pid) {\n  if (!Number.isInteger(pid) || pid <= 0) return false;\n  try {\n    process.kill(pid, 0);\n    return true;\n  } catch (e) {\n    return e && e.code === 'EPERM';\n  }\n}\n\n// --- Acquire the lock atomically. Only one process wins per cluster. ---\nlet haveLock = false;\ntry {\n  const fd = fs.openSync(LOCK_FILE, 'wx'); // fails if the file already exists\n  fs.writeSync(fd, String(process.pid));\n  fs.closeSync(fd);\n  haveLock = true;\n} catch (e) {\n  // Lock exists. If its owner is alive, skip regardless of age. Only reclaim a\n  // dead owner (or an old lock with no readable owner PID).\n  let ownerPid = null;\n  try {\n    ownerPid = Number.parseInt(fs.readFileSync(LOCK_FILE, 'utf8').trim(), 10);\n  } catch (_) {}\n  if (isProcessAlive(ownerPid)) process.exit(0);\n\n  let stale = false;\n  try { stale = (Date.now() - fs.statSync(LOCK_FILE).mtimeMs) >= LOCK_STALE_MS; }\n  catch (_) { stale = true; } // lock vanished between open and stat — race; treat as reclaimable\n  if (!stale) process.exit(0);\n  try {\n    fs.unlinkSync(LOCK_FILE);\n    const fd = fs.openSync(LOCK_FILE, 'wx');\n    fs.writeSync(fd, String(process.pid));\n    fs.closeSync(fd);\n    haveLock = true;\n  }\n  catch (_) { process.exit(0); }\n}\n\nfunction releaseLock() {\n  if (haveLock) { try { fs.unlinkSync(LOCK_FILE); } catch (_) {} haveLock = false; }\n}\n\ntry {\n  const res = spawnSync('memsearch', ['index', ...existing], {\n    // Avoid an intermediate cmd.exe. On timeout, the direct child is killed\n    // instead of leaving an orphaned Python indexer behind.\n    shell: false,\n    windowsHide: true,\n    encoding: 'utf8',\n    // Runaway guard, not a budget. We are detached, so nothing is waiting on\n    // this. A full day's backlog measured 278s on 2026-09-02, so 600s means a\n    // timeout here is a genuine hang worth escalating - not normal work being\n    // cut off, which is what the old 45s cap was doing on every session end.\n    timeout: 600000,\n  });\n\n  const out = `${res.stdout || ''}${res.stderr || ''}`.trim();\n  try { fs.writeFileSync(INDEX_LOG, out ? out + '\\n' : '(no output)\\n'); } catch (_) {}\n\n  // Transient, self-healing failures (the next incremental index catches up)\n  // escalate to .errors.log only after a consecutive streak. Two classes:\n  // ETIMEDOUT (session-end contention) and Milvus-unreachable (Docker Desktop\n  // hasn't started yet — morning scheduled sessions routinely end before the\n  // Milvus containers are up; observed 2026-07-16 07:40, self-healed by 08:43).\n  function bumpTransientStreak(label, detail) {\n    let streak = 0;\n    try { streak = parseInt(fs.readFileSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE, 'utf8'), 10) || 0; } catch (_) {}\n    streak += 1;\n    try { fs.writeFileSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE, String(streak)); } catch (_) {}\n    if (streak >= TIMEOUT_STREAK_THRESHOLD) {\n      logErr(`index ${label} ${streak}x consecutively — indexing may not be keeping up: ${detail.slice(0, 200)}`);\n    } else {\n      try { fs.appendFileSync(INDEX_LOG, `(${label} — streak ${streak}/${TIMEOUT_STREAK_THRESHOLD}, next session catches up)\\n`); } catch (_) {}\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (res.error && res.error.code === 'ETIMEDOUT') {\n    bumpTransientStreak('ETIMEDOUT', res.error.message);\n  } else if (res.error) {\n    logErr(`index ${res.error.code || 'error'}: ${res.error.message.slice(0, 300)}`);\n    try { fs.unlinkSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE); } catch (_) {}\n  } else if (res.status !== 0 && /Fail connecting to server/i.test(out)) {\n    bumpTransientStreak('Milvus unreachable', (res.stderr || out));\n  } else if (res.status !== 0 && LOCK_BUSY_RE.test(out)) {\n    // Another writer (nightly reindex, a second session, a manual index) holds\n    // milvus.db/LOCK. Transient by definition — the next incremental run covers\n    // this session's content. Previously this fell through to the hard-error\n    // branch and spammed .errors.log; worse, milvus-lite words the lock error\n    // as \"may not be compatible with Milvus Lite 3.x\", which has already caused\n    // one destructive rebuild. Never treat this as corruption.\n    bumpTransientStreak('index busy (lock held)', (res.stderr || out));\n  } else if (res.status !== 0) {\n    logErr(`index exit ${res.status}: ${(res.stderr || '').slice(0, 300)}`);\n    try { fs.unlinkSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE); } catch (_) {}\n  } else {\n    // Clean run — reset the consecutive-timeout streak.\n    try { fs.unlinkSync(TIMEOUT_STREAK_FILE); } catch (_) {}\n  }\n} finally {\n  releaseLock();\n}\n\nprocess.exit(0);\n---\nname: memsearch-hook-timeout-cap\ndescription: \"the SessionEnd memsearch hook's fixed 45s cap was outrun by corpus growth, so every session-end index timed out and incremental recall silently degraded to the 09:00 nightly; fixed 2026-09-02 by detaching the indexer\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  modified: 2026-09-02T12:33:29.526Z\n  originSessionId: 00fe7fc2-f53c-471d-b407-c777e8cc679c\n---\n\n`~/.claude/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` (SessionEnd) ran `memsearch index <10 paths>` through\n`spawnSync` with a **45s cap**, sized in July against a \"~17s fixed floor, ~5s incremental\"\nmeasurement. Measured again 2026-09-02 against the current corpus (~1,450 files;\n`SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings` alone is 593 and `SecondBrain/Knowledge` 334):\n\n| run | time |\n|---|---|\n| no-op, nothing new to embed | **33s** (was ~17s) |\n| one day of accumulated content | **278s** (232 chunks, ~1s/chunk) |\n\nSo the cap left ~12s of embedding budget — under a dozen new chunks. **Every ordinary\nsession-end index was timing out.** Incremental indexing had quietly degraded to \"whatever\nthe 09:00 nightly full reindex picks up\", leaving search up to 24h stale.\n\n**Why it stayed invisible for weeks.** Three dampers stacked:\n`daily-backup.ps1` resets `.memsearch-timeout-streak` every morning, the escalation\nthreshold is 6 consecutive timeouts, and the hook writes only to `.memsearch-index.log`\nbelow that. So a normal day of 2-4 session-ends never reached the threshold and never\nsurfaced. It took **six session-ends inside 47 minutes** (2026-09-01, 21:09-21:56) to\nproduce a 7-long streak and one `.errors.log` line. The alarm fired on burst *frequency*,\nnever on the underlying constant failure.\n\n**The misread that line invites:** ETIMEDOUT + a clustered burst pattern-matches\n[[milvus-lock-misdiagnosis]] (session-end lock contention), and the hook's own comments\nsay exactly that. It was not contention — `pgrep`/`lsof` showed no holder and the DB was\nhealthy. **Time the actual command before believing the comment.**\n\n**Fix (2026-09-02): detached background worker.** The hook now re-execs itself as\n`node memsearch-sync.js --worker` with `detached: true, stdio: 'ignore'` and `.unref()`,\nthen exits — measured **42ms**. The worker keeps the entire original body, so it owns the\nPID lock (clustered session-ends still elect one indexer), writes `INDEX_LOG`, and runs\nevery error branch. Its `spawnSync` cap is now 600s: a runaway guard, not a budget, so an\nETIMEDOUT there genuinely means \">10 minutes\".\n\n- `stdio: 'ignore'` is **not optional** — inherited or piped stdio keeps the harness's pipe\n  open and it waits for the child anyway, rebuilding the timeout with extra steps.\n- Self-respawn, **not** an `sh -c` wrapper: the wrapper would discard the lock ownership\n  and all four error-classification branches.\n- The old header justified staying synchronous with a **Windows** process-group race.\n  That rationale died with the 2026-08-05 migration; the comment now says so, because the\n  next reader would otherwise \"restore\" the bug.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- A fixed timeout sized against a measured corpus is a **dated** number. When the corpus is\n  something that grows every day (the vault, meetings, dailies), the cap has an expiry date\n  nobody writes down. Prefer detaching over raising a cap; if a cap must stay, record the\n  measurement and the date beside it so drift is visible.\n- Never size a SessionEnd hook to block. Session teardown cannot wait minutes, so any\n  design where \"correct\" means minutes must be asynchronous.\n- Both harnesses wire their own copy. `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` is now a","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"02f49de8-71d4-4aff-a438-3cff73adab28","session_id":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/sleep-scheduler","sessionId":"b4e73986-d9fe-48ee-8449-471485f44b0e","version":"2.1.273","gitBranch":"main"}
